WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
A newborn neuron must cross a wall many times its own size.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:10.400
It has no legs. How does it get across?

3
00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.820
The wall has layers.

4
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.640
Neurons are born in the ventricular zone, on the inside.

5
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.140
Next come the subventricular and intermediate zones.

6
00:00:21.233 --> 00:00:23.613
Then the cortical plate, where neurons settle.

7
00:00:24.413 --> 00:00:27.273
The marginal zone sits on top, just under the surface.

8
00:00:28.317 --> 00:00:30.517
A neuron's birth date predicts its layer.

9
00:00:31.437 --> 00:00:35.037
Early-born neurons settle deep, later-born ones nearer the surface.

10
00:00:35.917 --> 00:00:37.337
So every neuron must travel.

11
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.810
A migrating neuron has a leading process, one long arm pointing the way.

12
00:00:43.730 --> 00:00:46.430
Behind it is the cell body, which holds the nucleus.

13
00:00:47.483 --> 00:00:52.343
Near the nucleus sits the centrosome, the cell's main organizing centre for

14
00:00:52.343 --> 00:00:53.343
microtubules.

15
00:00:54.033 --> 00:00:59.453
In 1972, Pasko Rakic rebuilt young neurons from fetal monkey cortex.

16
00:01:00.453 --> 00:01:04.813
He found them pressed along fibres of radial glia, long cells spanning the wall.

17
00:01:05.817 --> 00:01:07.597
But fixed tissue shows contact.

18
00:01:08.537 --> 00:01:10.437
It cannot show which cell moved, or how.

19
00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.480
In 1987, James Edmondson and Mary Hatten filmed living neurons moving on glial

20
00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:18.480
fibres in a dish.

21
00:01:19.433 --> 00:01:21.773
The neurons moved in a stop-and-go rhythm.

22
00:01:22.693 --> 00:01:26.013
The tip reached forward and pulled back, out of step with the cell body.

23
00:01:27.067 --> 00:01:32.267
Bruce Schaar and Susan McConnell then watched single cycles in neuroblasts, young

24
00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.447
migrating neurons, from newborn rats.

25
00:01:35.347 --> 00:01:36.807
These are not cortical neurons.

26
00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.360
A swelling forms in the leading process, ahead of the nucleus.

27
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:45.160
Which moves into it first, the centrosome or the nucleus?

28
00:01:51.583 --> 00:01:53.283
The centrosome goes first.

29
00:01:54.403 --> 00:01:58.323
Cells fixed at this moment show it ahead, and later movies of cortical neurons

30
00:01:58.323 --> 00:02:01.003
tracked it. Then the nucleus jumps forward.

31
00:02:05.416 --> 00:02:06.796
Here is the one idea.

32
00:02:07.656 --> 00:02:10.296
In the typical bipolar climb, the front goes first.

33
00:02:11.196 --> 00:02:13.856
The cell has moved only when its nucleus catches up.

34
00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:17.650
A tip can keep growing while the nucleus stays put.

35
00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:21.170
So track the nucleus against the tissue, not the tip.

36
00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.733
Count the steps of the nucleus.

37
00:02:23.733 --> 00:02:29.313
6 micrometres a step, 4 steps an hour, gives 24 micrometres an hour.

38
00:02:33.616 --> 00:02:35.436
Follow the nucleus, not the tip.
